s. It is applied in the rapid-recrystallization region and in the non-recrystallization region, and it influences apparent gamma-grain-boundary area, gamma-grain-boundary area, r3 temperature, and locally very coarse grains [Data: Entities (229, 410, 546, 547, 654); Relationships (345, 985, 988, 1331, 758)]. This makes reduction scheduling a key control lever in the community's controlled-rolling framework.\"},{\"summary\":\"Recovery region produces huge grains through slight reduction and boundary migration\",\"explanation\":\"The RECOVERY REGION is where repeated deformation leads to sluggish and incomplete recrystallization and where initial rolling passes can form huge grains locally. A slight reduction in the recovery region can form HUGE GRAINS that persist even after many passes in the partial-recrystallization region, and strain-induced grain-boundary migration causes locally very coarse grains [Data: Entities (410, 430, 432, 421, 417); Relationships (730, 732, 733, 767, 761)].\"},{\"summary\":\"Huge grains and coarse ferrite reduce toughness\",\"explanation\":\"HUGE GRAINS are associated with coarse ferrite grains that give poor toughness. POOR TOUGHNESS is exhibited by fully acicular ferrite, upper bainite and/or coarse ferrite grains formed from huge grains, and bainitic structures in medium hardenability steel when controlled rolling is not used [Data: Entities (421, 422, 331); Relationships (737, 528)]. This links microstructural coarsening directly to an undesirable mechanical property.\"},{\"summary\":\"Rolling procedures integrate reduction, delay, and final reduction\",\"explanation\":\"ROLLING PROCEDURES involve reduction in the rapid-recrystallization region, delay in rolling, and final reduction in the non-recrystallization region. The Baird and Preston interpretation describes this sequence, and delay in rolling ensures the required amount of deformation in the non-recrystallization region [Data: Entities (231, 227, 240, 253, 228); Relationships (293, 299, 344, 372)]. This procedural combination defines the processing path for achieving desired microstructures.\"},{\"summary\":\"Partial and complete recrystallization govern grain growth behavior\",\"explanation\":\"After partial recrystallization, austenite grain growth is slowed. In the partial-recrystallization region, recrystallization volume fraction increases with passes, while once complete recrystallization takes place, successive recrystallization proceeds much more easily [Data: Entities (411, 424, 431, 1281); Relationships (2747, 764, 739)]. These recrystallization states control whether grains refine or coarsen during rolling.\"},{\"summary\":\"Microstructural change is both a measurement route and a mechanism input\",\"explanation\":\"MICROSTRUCTURAL CHANGE affects the microstructure and is one technique used to measure the strain-induced Ar3 temperature. It is also considered when presenting strengthening and toughening mechanisms [Data: Entities (602, 1202, 632, 643); Relationships (1141, 2608, 1241)]. This dual role makes it a bridge between characterization and property optimization.\"},{\"summary\":\"Strain-induced Ar3 temperature is measured by several methods\",\"explanation\":\"Strain-induced Ar3 temperature can be measured by thermal analysis, dilatometric methods with a compression device, microstructural change, and texture intensity; these measurements were not always precise or under real controlled-rolling conditions [Data: Entities (612, 613, 639, 602, 631); Relationships (1193, 1194, 1196, 1241, 1198)]. The compression device is specifically used in dilatometric methods. This finding highlights both methodological diversity and measurement limitations.\"},{\"summary\":\"Gamma grain-boundary area responds to reduction level\",\"explanation\":\"Apparent gamma-grain-boundary area, which includes gamma-grain boundaries plus deformation bands, increases with reduction and reaches a steady level above about 30% reduction. Gamma-grain-